+---
+name: freebsd-port
+description: >-
+  Create a new FreeBSD port or update an existing one in a fresh clone
+  of the public ports tree, lint it, and post it to lab-devel as a
+  [freebsd-ports PATCH] for review. Prepares the patch only; the port
+  is never built.
+disable-model-invocation: true
+argument-hint: <category/port> [version, home page, repository, ...]
+---
+
+# freebsd-port
+
+Create or update one FreeBSD port, as asked:
+
+    $ARGUMENTS
+
+The arguments name the port's origin, `category/port`, and may add
+free-form detail such as the version, home page, or upstream
+repository. If the origin exists in the tree, the job is an update;
+otherwise it is a new port. If they do not name an origin, or give too
+little to find the software, ask before starting.
+
+The result is a patch on lab-devel. Pat builds it with poudriere and
+takes it upstream, so this skill prepares the patch and nothing more.
+spec.org beside this file is the specification; this file is how to
+carry it out.
+
+## 1. Check the tools
+
+    for t in git portclippy portfmt portlint; do
+        command -v "${t}" >/dev/null || echo "missing: ${t}"
+    done
+
+portclippy and portfmt come from ports-mgmt/portfmt, portlint from
+ports-mgmt/portlint. If any is missing, stop and say so. A patch that
+has not been linted is not ready to post.
+
+## 2. Get an up-to-date ports tree
+
+The tree is a git clone of the public upstream, kept in /var/tmp
+between runs. A blobless clone fetches file history only when it is
+read, which keeps the first clone small:
+
+    tree=/var/tmp/freebsd-ports
+    origin="category/port"
+    if [ ! -d "${tree}/.git" ]; then
+        git clone --filter=blob:none \
+            https://github.com/freebsd/freebsd-ports.git "${tree}"
+    fi
+    cd "${tree}"
+    git fetch -q origin
+    git reset -q --hard
+    git clean -fdq
+    git switch -q -C "$(echo "${origin}" | tr / -)" origin/main
+
+The tree is scratch space that belongs to this skill. The list is the
+record of the work, so anything an earlier run left behind is either
+posted already or abandoned, and is discarded.
+
+## 3. Research
+
+The ports tree is thousands of working examples, and any problem the
+port raises has almost certainly been solved in it already. Use the
+framework; do not invent. It is better to stop with a question than to
+post a solution the ports community will reject.
+
+- For an update, read the port's files and its history,
+  `git log -p -- "${origin}"`. Earlier commits show the port's
+  conventions and where its changelogs live.
+- Find ports built the same way (the same `USES`, the same language
+  ecosystem) and follow them. Where the tree offers several mechanisms
+  for one task, dependencies especially, count how many comparable
+  ports use each and follow the one most use now. A mechanism only a
+  handful of ports still use is a warning sign, not a template. When
+  the standard mechanism hits an obstacle, find how other ports on it
+  solved the same obstacle before falling back.
+- The Porter's Handbook is the reference:
+  https://docs.freebsd.org/en/books/porters-handbook/
+
+src/portia/lore.org records lessons from earlier porting work, such
+as the Go port that broke on a mechanism the tree had abandoned. Read
+it when a choice is not obvious.
+
+## 4. Choose the latest good version
+
+Use the newest released version, checked against upstream's release
+page or the language registry (PyPI, crates.io, Hex, npm), not only
+the repository's tags. A tag is not a release.
+
+- Exclude yanked versions. Registries call it yank, retract, unlist,
+  unpublish, or retire; a withdrawn version still fetches, so check
+  its status.
+- Exclude pre-releases: alpha, beta, rc, dev, and anything upstream
+  marks as a pre-release.
+
+For an update, collect the changelog link of every version between the
+port's version and the new one. If the port is already at the latest
+good version, stop and say so; there is nothing to post.
+
+## 5. Write the port
+
+A port is `Makefile`, `distinfo`, `pkg-descr`, and a plist
+(`pkg-plist`, `PLIST_FILES`, or one the framework generates). Keep the
+Makefile in the order the handbook gives.
+
+- Take dependency bounds from upstream's own list (`Cargo.toml`,
+  `go.mod`, `pyproject.toml`, `mix.exs`) and express them in
+  `*_DEPENDS`. Where the tree cannot satisfy a bound, loosen it to what
+  the tree provides and say so in the commit message.
+- If upstream ships tests, hook them into the `test` target with the
+  knob the port's `USES` provides (`CARGO_TEST`, `GO_TESTTARGET`,
+  `USE_PYTHON=pytest`, `USES=cmake:testing`, `TEST_TARGET`). A
+  hand-written `do-test` silently replaces the framework's. Test-only
+  dependencies go in `TEST_DEPENDS`.
+- For an update, reset `PORTREVISION` and regenerate `distinfo`.
+
+Do not build; it takes too long, and poudriere checks the build. Reading
+metadata is fine. Fetch, extract, and the framework's metadata targets
+are allowed; configure, build, stage, install, and check-plist are
+not. Keep distfiles, work directories, and saved options out of the
+tree and out of the root-owned defaults, and take default options
+without prompting:
+
+    portmake() {
+        make -C "${tree}/${origin}" PORTSDIR="${tree}" BATCH=yes \
+            DISTDIR=/var/tmp/freebsd-ports-distfiles \
+            WRKDIRPREFIX=/var/tmp/freebsd-ports-work \
+            PORT_DBDIR=/var/tmp/freebsd-ports-db "${@}"
+    }
+    portmake makesum # writes distinfo from the fetched distfiles
+    portmake extract # to read upstream's sources
+    portmake cargo-crates # or gomod-vendor, as the framework requires
+
+Never write a checksum by hand. Without a build the plist cannot be
+checked, so draft it from upstream's install rules and say in the patch
+notes that it is unverified.
+
+## 6. Lint
+
+    cd "${tree}/${origin}"
+    portclippy Makefile
+    portfmt -D Makefile
+    PORTSDIR="${tree}" portlint -A
+
+`portfmt -D` prints the diff it would apply; make the changes it
+suggests by hand. Fix every finding, or explain in the patch notes why
+it stays.
+
+## 7. Commit
+
+One port per commit. Related ports, such as a new dependency and the
+port that needs it, form a series with the dependency first.
+
+    cd "${tree}"
+    git add -A "${origin}"
+    git commit -q -F /tmp/freebsd-port.msg
+
+Write the message to that file first, following the ports tree's
+conventions, with lines wrapped at 72 columns. A new port:
+
+    category/port: <COMMENT from the Makefile>
+
+    <pkg-descr, edited to fit>
+
+    WWW: <WWW from the Makefile>
+
+An update:
+
+    category/port: Update 1.0 => 1.2
+
+    Changelogs:
+    https://example.org/1.1
+    https://example.org/1.2
+
+Leave out the `PR:` trailer. No bug exists yet; Pat adds it when he
+files one.
+
+## 8. Post
+
+The patch is applied with `git am` in a ports tree, not with lab's
+`just am`, so it is formatted with plain git and carries the
+`[freebsd-ports PATCH]` prefix:
+
+    base="$(git rev-parse origin/main)"
+    rm -rf /tmp/freebsd-port
+    git format-patch -o /tmp/freebsd-port --thread=shallow \
+        --subject-prefix="freebsd-ports PATCH" --base="${base}" \
+        --to="Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>" --cc="lab-devel@p7x.dev" \
+        origin/main..HEAD
+
+For more than one patch add `--cover-letter` and fill it in. Below the
+`---` of a single patch, or in the cover letter, write the notes for
+review: the ports you followed, what was checked (lint, makesum), what
+was not (the build, the plist), and any loosened dependency bound.
+
+Deliver the series into the mail workspace and push it, as
+zib/patch-review-workflow.org describes:
+
+    for p in /tmp/freebsd-port/*.patch; do
+        ~/lab/default.jj/libexec/mail/maildir-deliver.sh < "${p}"
+    done
+    cd ~/lab/mail.jj
+    jj diff --summary # only "A maildir/..." lines
+    jj commit -m "msg: [freebsd-ports PATCH] <subject>"
+    jj bookmark set "$(whoami)" -r @-
+    jj git push --remote submissions --bookmark "$(whoami)"
+
+When a list message asked for the port, reply to it and cite the patch
+by its full subject, with its Message-ID in a footnote.
